<poml>
    <task>
        Investigate a robot log to find and extract the moments that matter, using
        the fewest and cheapest tool calls that answer the user's question.
        <stepwise-instructions>
            <list listStyle="decimal">
                <item>
                    Start cheap: call `describe_data_source` on the path. This is
                    inexpensive even for very large files. Note the topics, time
                    range, and message counts. If the source needs extra arguments
                    (a CAN capture needs `args={"dbc": "./path/to/file.dbc"}`), a
                    typed error will say so - fix the arguments rather than
                    retrying blindly.
                </item>
                <item>
                    If the source is CSV/JSON and the metadata reports a non-zero
                    `excluded_file_count`, tell the user some files were excluded
                    as invalid before drawing conclusions - an empty-looking
                    result may be missing data, not absent events.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Call `describe_topic` on the one or two topics most relevant
                    to the question to learn the DuckDB schema, field paths, and
                    units. Topic columns are DuckDB `STRUCT`s: refer to nested
                    fields as `topic['field']['subfield']`.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Query narrow time windows, not the whole log: pass
                    `start_seconds`/`end_seconds` to `query_messages` whenever the
                    question allows it. Iterate: a coarse aggregate first (COUNT,
                    MIN/MAX, bucketed averages) to locate interesting regions,
                    then detailed queries inside those regions.
                </item>
                <item>
                    When you have found the moments that matter, extract evidence
                    around them instead of describing numbers: a snippet (one clip
                    per event) or a GIF for camera topics. Report each artifact's
                    output path to the user.
                </item>
                <item>
                    If the investigation turns into "keep windows around every
                    such event", switch to the pipeline capability
                    (`compose/pipeline`) instead of extracting clips one at a
                    time; for message-latency questions, the `diagnose/latency`
                    capability has the recipe.
                </item>
            </list>
        </stepwise-instructions>
    </task>
    <output-format>
        Findings first (what happened and when), then the evidence artifacts with
        their paths, then the queries used - so the user can rerun or refine them.
    </output-format>
</poml>
